WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.189 align:start position:0%
 
financial markets have been on an  absolute tear over the last 12 months  everything seems to be going up over and  over and over again over the weekend i  was speaking at a conference and one of  the things that came up was everyone was  saying why should i go ahead and look at  other assets if my portfolio was going  up well when you start to think about a  portfolio and i asked you how did your  portfolio do over the last 12 months  what you would quote me is the nominal  rate of return you would say well the s  p 500 is up 29 over the last 12 months  so my portfolio was up 29 but if i asked  you what the real rate of return is you  would take that nominal number 29 and  you would subtract the six plus percent  inflation so actually you were doing  somewhere around  22.5  on a real return basis sounds pretty  good right no here is one of the big  things that i think most people are  going to start to wrap their heads  around is that when you are quoting the  financial return of your portfolio  you're comparing it to dollars it's a  medium of exchange it's a currency that  dollar is not a fixed amount of value  instead that dollar is actually being  devalued at a historic rate right now  that's where you get the 6  plus inflation and so what you're doing  is you're taking the asset itself the  one share the one piece of property the  one commodity and you are denominating  it in a depreciating value if the stock  market went up 29  how much of the 29 gain in the stock  market is because the dollar has been  devaluing or the stock market is  actually creating and capturing upside  value you can't really measure it and so  ultimately what i think more and more  people are going to do and what i  personally have started to do is i  denominate my entire portfolio in  bitcoin why well bitcoin is a finite  supply asset its inflation rate right  now is 1.78 that is lower than anything  else from a currency perspective that is  considered a major currency that people  would denominate their portfolios and so  if you go ahead and you compare bitcoin  and let's say the s p 500 if we  denominated over the entire lifetime  bitcoin  denominated in the s p here we go it has  fallen off a cliff the s p 500 has been  crashing the s p 500 is negative when  denominated in bitcoin remember this is  a fix supply asset that is a  programmatic monetary policy that no one  can manipulate what you are looking at  right here is an absolute destruction of  the s p 500 value in a portfolio when  compared with a digital store of value  we then go ahead and we look over the  last year you can see that they're also  same exact story it has been falling  very very rapidly now many people will  say why should i think about this in  terms of bitcoin being the denomination  of my portfolio it's not an argument for  you to go and take majority of your  portfolio and put it into bitcoin rather  it's the exact opposite it is the  perspective that says if i want to  maintain the purchasing power of the  digital store of value then i need to  outperform bitcoin so some people go and  they will just buy the store value other  people will actually attempt to  outperform it and so when you go ahead  and you look at that you can compare it  to the historical example of people will  look at the dollar they'll say they're  six percent inflation i have to beat six  percent to actually be able to gain more  dollars well when it comes to bitcoin  bitcoin has been going up a lot for a  long time it's up over 300 over the last  12 months and it has compounded annually  for a decade at 180 percent 180  compound annual growth rate for a decade  which means that if your portfolio has  not been able to achieve 180 compound  annual growth rate then you quite  literally have been losing purchasing  power when measured by the digital store  value of bitcoin and so whether you look  at stocks whether you look at  commodities whether you look at bonds or  any other asset what you find is that  denominating your portfolio in dollars  continues to go up and up and up why  well this is true of every single  country that has high levels of  inflation many years there are the best  performing stock markets located in  venezuela zimbabwe argentina or other  places with high levels of inflation  because quite literally the currency in  which the stocks are denominated is  rapidly losing value it looks like the  stock prices are exploding but it's not  because necessarily the companies are  becoming more valuable it's because the  currencies are being devalued if you  were to take the stock market and  denominate it in gold as well the stock  market does not look nearly as  attractive when it starts to be  denominated in terms of an asset that is  not manipulated from a supply  perspective and so ultimately i think  that people are gonna have to wake up to  the fact that denominating your wealth  denominating your portfolio in some sort  of sound money principle looking at it  and saying what is the thing that no one  can change no one can debase no one can  take away that supply that programmatic  transparent monetary policy and that my  friends is why so many people feel like  i'm getting rich on paper but i can't  get ahead well it's because actually if  you were to look at the unofficial uh  inflation numbers it's not six percent  it's closer to 15 and so if you're not  delivering a 15 plus return on your  portfolio then you literally are falling  behind the unofficial inflation number  and if you were to look at bitcoin you'd  have to be delivering over 300  over the last 12 months

